<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#columbian-exchange">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Columbian exchange</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as New World crops</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Columbian Exchange is the transfer of plants, animals, diseases and people between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the rest of the world following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s voyages from 1492. American crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> transformed nutrition worldwide, while Old World diseases devastated indigenous populations. It was a major consequence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, and New World crops supported population growth in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange.jpg" alt="Columbian exchange" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VegCorn_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term was first used in 1972 by the American historian Alfred W. Crosby in his book The Columbian Exchange, which mapped the biological and cultural transfers between the Old and New Worlds and was rapidly adopted by other historians. Before 1492 there is little evidence of contact between the two hemispheres. The Norse visits to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and Vinland in the late 10th and 11th centuries had no known impact on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and many scientists accept that contact between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> and coastal South Americans around 1200 explains the sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. The exchange began with the first voyage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> in 1492.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Plants moved in both directions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">Potatoes</a>, grown only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> before 1500, were widely eaten in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by the 18th century and are estimated to have contributed 12 to 25 per cent of the population growth in Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> between 1700 and 1900. The Portuguese brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in the 16th century, and it gradually replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> as the most important food crop there. Spanish colonisers brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and the Portuguese took chili <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">peppers</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the other direction <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane, citrus fruits and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> became important in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and enslaved Africans brought rice-growing skills to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>.</p><p>Horses, donkeys, mules, pigs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep, goats, chickens and bees were rapidly adopted by native peoples; the Plains Indians used horses for hunting and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> adopted them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arauco-war">Arauco War</a>. The turkey and the Muscovy duck went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Diseases were far deadlier toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Hispaniola's pre-contact population was probably at least 500,000, and fewer than 500 remained by 1526. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a>, first recorded in the Americas in 1518, is estimated to have killed 40 per cent of the 200,000 people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in 1520.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>An estimated 80 to 95 per cent of the Native American population died in epidemics within 100 to 150 years of 1492. The Mexican population fell from an estimated 20 million to barely more than a million in the 16th century. The need for labour and the decline of Indigenous peoples was linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, which brought 11.7 million Africans to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, far more than the about 3.4 million Europeans who migrated between 1492 and 1840. The Old World also gained from the discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinine">quinine</a>, the first effective treatment for malaria. Syphilis may have been carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s crew, though historians debate its origin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Columbian exchange” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economy rested on agriculture: rice, wheat and millet, supplemented by cotton, sericulture and, from the late 16th century, <b>New World crops</b> such as maize and sweet potatoes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>This growth was aided by long internal peace and by <b>New World crops</b>, particularly maize, sweet potatoes and peanuts, which extended cultivation into previously marginal hill and upland territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Columbian Exchange</b>, the transfer of crops, animals, pathogens and peoples between the Old World and the Americas, was the most consequential and irreversible result of the empire's expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
